1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,260
<font color="#FFFFFF">StyleMorph is a 3D-aware generative model that
pulls apart the four things that make an</font>

2
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:08,945
<font color="#FFFFFF">image what it is: the object's 3D shape,</font>

3
00:00:09,025 --> 00:00:13,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">the camera pose, the object's appearance, and the
background.</font>

4
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:18,544
<font color="#FFFFFF">It does this by learning a 3D morphable model from
nothing but ordinary 2D photos,</font>

5
00:00:18,624 --> 00:00:23,042
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no 3D scans, no pose labels, and no template
supplied by hand.</font>

6
00:00:23,122 --> 00:00:30,493
<font color="#FFFFFF">The trick is to morph a single canonical 3D
template and render its coordinates into a purely geometric 2D map,</font>

7
00:00:30,573 --> 00:00:35,259
<font color="#FFFFFF">which then conditions a StyleGAN renderer for
photorealistic synthesis.</font>

8
00:00:35,339 --> 00:00:37,876
<font color="#FFFFFF">On faces, cats, dogs, and wild animals,</font>

9
00:00:37,956 --> 00:00:43,179
<font color="#FFFFFF">StyleMorph matches state-of-the-art image quality
while giving you independent,</font>

10
00:00:43,259 --> 00:00:46,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">fine-grained control over every factor of variation.</font>

11
00:00:47,050 --> 00:00:51,224
<font color="#FFFFFF">The dream in 3D-aware image generation is a model
you can steer,</font>

12
00:00:51,304 --> 00:00:54,946
<font color="#FFFFFF">one where you change the pose without touching identity,</font>

13
00:00:55,026 --> 00:00:58,469
<font color="#FFFFFF">or swap the background without disturbing the object.</font>

14
00:00:58,549 --> 00:01:01,726
<font color="#FFFFFF">Today's best 3D-aware GANs make beautiful images,</font>

15
00:01:01,806 --> 00:01:05,914
<font color="#FFFFFF">but their hybrid design fuses geometry and
appearance together,</font>

16
00:01:05,994 --> 00:01:08,905
<font color="#FFFFFF">so editing one factor bleeds into the others.</font>

17
00:01:08,985 --> 00:01:13,292
<font color="#FFFFFF">Classical 3D morphable models solve the control
problem elegantly,</font>

18
00:01:13,372 --> 00:01:18,078
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet they require expensive 3D scanning and
painstaking manual alignment,</font>

19
00:01:18,158 --> 00:01:21,202
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is why they mainly exist for human faces.</font>

20
00:01:21,282 --> 00:01:27,251
<font color="#FFFFFF">StyleMorph asks whether we can get morphable-model
control for arbitrary object categories,</font>

21
00:01:27,331 --> 00:01:30,840
<font color="#FFFFFF">learning everything from unstructured 2D images alone.</font>

22
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:37,984
<font color="#FFFFFF">Morphable models are the workhorse of visual
effects and augmented reality because they hand creators clean,</font>

23
00:01:38,064 --> 00:01:41,279
<font color="#FFFFFF">separate dials for pose, expression, and appearance.</font>

24
00:01:41,359 --> 00:01:48,629
<font color="#FFFFFF">The question that motivates this work is simple:
can we get that same level of control inside a modern 3D-aware GAN,</font>

25
00:01:48,709 --> 00:01:53,318
<font color="#FFFFFF">but without any of the 3D supervision that
morphable models normally need?</font>

26
00:01:53,398 --> 00:01:59,592
<font color="#FFFFFF">Prior work that added 3D deformations to synthesis
either stayed limited to a single dynamic scene,</font>

27
00:01:59,672 --> 00:02:04,218
<font color="#FFFFFF">or leaned on a deformable template that already
existed for the category.</font>

28
00:02:04,298 --> 00:02:11,441
<font color="#FFFFFF">StyleMorph instead turns the morphable model itself
into something the network discovers from unlabeled 2D images,</font>

29
00:02:11,521 --> 00:02:14,990
<font color="#FFFFFF">making it a first-class citizen of generative modelling.</font>

30
00:02:15,370 --> 00:02:19,112
<font color="#FFFFFF">StyleMorph makes three linked contributions.</font>

31
00:02:19,192 --> 00:02:22,933
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

32
00:02:23,013 --> 00:02:26,755
<font color="#FFFFFF">it learns a 3D morphable model of an object
category's non-rigid shape variation using nothing but 2D images,</font>

33
00:02:26,835 --> 00:02:30,576
<font color="#FFFFFF">by morphing a canonical template through backpropagation.</font>

34
00:02:30,656 --> 00:02:34,398
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it introduces Template Object Coordinates,</font>

35
00:02:34,478 --> 00:02:38,219
<font color="#FFFFFF">or TOCS: a deformable cousin of Normalized Object
Coordinates that gives every surface</font>

36
00:02:38,299 --> 00:02:42,041
<font color="#FFFFFF">point a stable template identity and acts as a powerful,</font>

37
00:02:42,121 --> 00:02:45,862
<font color="#FFFFFF">deformation-equivariant descriptor of shape.</font>

38
00:02:45,942 --> 00:02:49,684
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

39
00:02:49,764 --> 00:02:53,505
<font color="#FFFFFF">it feeds 2D TOCS maps as a purely geometric
conditioning signal into a StyleGAN-based deferred neural renderer,</font>

40
00:02:53,585 --> 00:02:57,327
<font color="#FFFFFF">cleanly separating shape from appearance.</font>

41
00:02:57,407 --> 00:03:01,148
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together these deliver disentangled control over
pose, shape, object appearance,</font>

42
00:03:01,228 --> 00:03:04,970
<font color="#FFFFFF">and background, all at high resolution.</font>

43
00:03:05,350 --> 00:03:06,971
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method has two halves.</font>

44
00:03:07,051 --> 00:03:08,344
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the geometry side,</font>

45
00:03:08,424 --> 00:03:14,035
<font color="#FFFFFF">a Morphable Renderer learns a deformation-free
object in canonical template coordinates</font>

46
00:03:14,115 --> 00:03:16,128
<font color="#FFFFFF">as a constant implicit function.</font>

47
00:03:16,208 --> 00:03:19,791
<font color="#FFFFFF">A SIREN-based deformation field, driven by a shape code,</font>

48
00:03:19,871 --> 00:03:25,875
<font color="#FFFFFF">warps each camera ray from world space into that
template space by adding a predicted offset.</font>

49
00:03:25,955 --> 00:03:31,631
<font color="#FFFFFF">Integrating the template coordinates along each ray
produces a 2D map called a TOCS map,</font>

50
00:03:31,711 --> 00:03:36,275
<font color="#FFFFFF">which encodes the compounded effects of shape
deformation, camera pose,</font>

51
00:03:36,355 --> 00:03:39,284
<font color="#FFFFFF">and perspective, and nothing about appearance.</font>

52
00:03:39,364 --> 00:03:40,723
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the synthesis side,</font>

53
00:03:40,803 --> 00:03:46,348
<font color="#FFFFFF">a StyleGAN2-based deferred neural renderer takes
this geometric TOCS map together with</font>

54
00:03:46,428 --> 00:03:48,507
<font color="#FFFFFF">two independent appearance codes,</font>

55
00:03:48,587 --> 00:03:52,628
<font color="#FFFFFF">one for the foreground object and one for the
background scene,</font>

56
00:03:52,708 --> 00:03:55,114
<font color="#FFFFFF">and generates a high-resolution image.</font>

57
00:03:55,194 --> 00:04:00,804
<font color="#FFFFFF">The foreground and background are composited with
an alpha mask predicted by the model.</font>

58
00:04:00,884 --> 00:04:06,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">Training proceeds in two stages: first the
deformable volume renderer learns realistic</font>

59
00:04:06,510 --> 00:04:10,158
<font color="#FFFFFF">shape at low resolution with weak silhouette supervision,</font>

60
00:04:10,238 --> 00:04:15,914
<font color="#FFFFFF">then it is frozen and used only to supply TOCS maps
while the 2D renderer is trained for</font>

61
00:04:15,994 --> 00:04:18,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">photorealistic full-resolution output.</font>

62
00:04:18,780 --> 00:04:22,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">StyleMorph is evaluated on four widely used image
datasets.</font>

63
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:30,455
<font color="#FFFFFF">FFHQ contributes seventy thousand centered human
face photos with a range of challenging backgrounds and poses.</font>

64
00:04:30,535 --> 00:04:36,401
<font color="#FFFFFF">The AFHQ collection adds animal faces in three
splits: roughly fifty-six hundred cats,</font>

65
00:04:36,481 --> 00:04:40,481
<font color="#FFFFFF">fifty-two hundred dogs, and fifty-two hundred wild
animals.</font>

66
00:04:40,561 --> 00:04:45,598
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors report Frechet Inception Distance at
two-fifty-six resolution,</font>

67
00:04:45,678 --> 00:04:50,092
<font color="#FFFFFF">comparing against eleven state-of-the-art 3D-aware
GAN baselines,</font>

68
00:04:50,172 --> 00:04:56,039
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the results test both photorealism on faces and
the ability to generalize to animal</font>

69
00:04:56,119 --> 00:04:59,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">categories that classical morphable models never covered.</font>

70
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:05,632
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline finding is that disentanglement does
not have to cost image quality.</font>

71
00:05:05,712 --> 00:05:11,315
<font color="#FFFFFF">StyleMorph reaches a Frechet Inception Distance of
seven point nine one on FFHQ faces,</font>

72
00:05:11,395 --> 00:05:16,006
<font color="#FFFFFF">and four point two nine on cats, three point four
nine on wild animals,</font>

73
00:05:16,086 --> 00:05:20,631
<font color="#FFFFFF">and thirteen point nine five on dogs, all at
two-fifty-six resolution.</font>

74
00:05:20,711 --> 00:05:27,371
<font color="#FFFFFF">These numbers sit right alongside the strongest
3D-aware GANs that offer none of StyleMorph's control.</font>

75
00:05:27,451 --> 00:05:31,270
<font color="#FFFFFF">The comparison that matters most is against
Disentangled3D,</font>

76
00:05:31,350 --> 00:05:37,151
<font color="#FFFFFF">the only prior method that also represents shape
with a deformable template: StyleMorph's</font>

77
00:05:37,231 --> 00:05:42,701
<font color="#FFFFFF">FFHQ score of seven point nine one is far ahead of
its twenty-eight point one eight,</font>

78
00:05:42,781 --> 00:05:46,996
<font color="#FFFFFF">and StyleMorph additionally separates foreground
from background,</font>

79
00:05:47,076 --> 00:05:48,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">which that method does not.</font>

80
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:52,887
<font color="#FFFFFF">The ablations, all run under a fair ninety-six-hour
training budget,</font>

81
00:05:52,967 --> 00:05:56,694
<font color="#FFFFFF">isolate each design choice.</font>

82
00:05:56,774 --> 00:06:00,501
<font color="#FFFFFF">Swapping TOCS for plain NOCS raises FID from about
eight point three to eight point nine</font>

83
00:06:00,581 --> 00:06:04,309
<font color="#FFFFFF">and blurs the conditioning signal,</font>

84
00:06:04,389 --> 00:06:08,116
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing template-space coordinates matter.</font>

85
00:06:08,196 --> 00:06:11,923
<font color="#FFFFFF">Removing the deformable module and using a direct
occupancy network instead keeps FID</font>

86
00:06:12,003 --> 00:06:15,730
<font color="#FFFFFF">similar but drastically worsens the consistency scores,</font>

87
00:06:15,810 --> 00:06:19,537
<font color="#FFFFFF">proving the value of modelling shape through deformation.</font>

88
00:06:19,617 --> 00:06:23,344
<font color="#FFFFFF">Switching from late fusion to early fusion nudges
FID down slightly but badly hurts alpha consistency,</font>

89
00:06:23,424 --> 00:06:27,151
<font color="#FFFFFF">letting foreground and background bleed together.</font>

90
00:06:27,231 --> 00:06:30,959
<font color="#FFFFFF">Finally,</font>

91
00:06:31,039 --> 00:06:34,766
<font color="#FFFFFF">directly optimizing view consistency as a
differentiable loss does improve that metric,</font>

92
00:06:34,846 --> 00:06:38,573
<font color="#FFFFFF">from nearly sixteen down to eight, but it trades
away image quality,</font>

93
00:06:38,653 --> 00:06:42,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">pushing FID up to twelve point three.</font>

94
00:06:42,760 --> 00:06:45,007
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

95
00:06:45,087 --> 00:06:49,167
<font color="#FFFFFF">On FFHQ faces StyleMorph scores a seven point nine
one FID,</font>

96
00:06:49,247 --> 00:06:54,879
<font color="#FFFFFF">versus twenty-eight point one eight for the only
competing template-based method.</font>

97
00:06:54,959 --> 00:07:00,590
<font color="#FFFFFF">On animals it reaches four point two nine on cats,
three point four nine on wild,</font>

98
00:07:00,670 --> 00:07:03,199
<font color="#FFFFFF">and thirteen point nine five on dogs.</font>

99
00:07:03,279 --> 00:07:09,122
<font color="#FFFFFF">It disentangles four independent factors: shape,
camera pose, foreground appearance,</font>

100
00:07:09,202 --> 00:07:10,956
<font color="#FFFFFF">and background appearance.</font>

101
00:07:11,036 --> 00:07:17,302
<font color="#FFFFFF">And the core TOCS-versus-NOCS ablation shows
template coordinates improving FID from eight</font>

102
00:07:17,382 --> 00:07:22,238
<font color="#FFFFFF">point nine to eight point three while also
sharpening disentanglement.</font>

103
00:07:22,318 --> 00:07:28,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">All of this is learned from unstructured 2D images,
with no 3D supervision at all.</font>

104
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:36,099
<font color="#FFFFFF">The one-line takeaway is that StyleMorph delivers
morphable-model control inside a state-of-the-art image generator,</font>

105
00:07:36,179 --> 00:07:38,849
<font color="#FFFFFF">learned entirely from ordinary 2D photos.</font>

106
00:07:38,929 --> 00:07:44,952
<font color="#FFFFFF">By morphing a learned canonical template and
rendering it into a purely geometric TOCS map,</font>

107
00:07:45,032 --> 00:07:49,646
<font color="#FFFFFF">it hands a StyleGAN renderer a clean geometric
signal, so shape, pose,</font>

108
00:07:49,726 --> 00:07:50,853
<font color="#FFFFFF">object appearance,</font>

109
00:07:50,933 --> 00:07:56,285
<font color="#FFFFFF">and background can each be edited independently
without sacrificing photorealism.</font>

110
00:07:56,365 --> 00:08:01,985
<font color="#FFFFFF">In effect it builds an unsupervised 3D morphable
model for general object categories,</font>

111
00:08:02,065 --> 00:08:08,558
<font color="#FFFFFF">extending the fine-grained controllability that
visual-effects artists rely on from faces to cats,</font>

112
00:08:08,638 --> 00:08:10,100
<font color="#FFFFFF">dogs, and wild animals.</font>
